The perpendicular height of an **isosceles triangle** comes from a right-angled triangle whose base is **half** the base of the triangle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc,decorations.markings}
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (0pt,-4pt) -- (0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4,0);
\coordinate (C) at (2,3);
\coordinate (M) at (2,0);
\draw[fill=blue!15] (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw[tickmark] (A)--(C);
\draw[tickmark] (B)--(C) node[midway,above right] {$s$};
\draw[dashed] (C)--(M) node[midway,right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$h$};
\coordinate (m1) at ($(M)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\coordinate (m2) at ($(M)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\draw (m1) -- ($(m1)+(m2)-(M)$) -- (m2);
\node[below] at (1,-0.05) {$\frac{b}{2}$};
\node[below] at (3,-0.05) {$\frac{b}{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In an isosceles trapezium each overhang is half the difference of the parallel sides.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (5,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4,2.6);
\coordinate (D) at (1,2.6);
\coordinate (P) at (1,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (4,0);
\draw[fill=blue!15] (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(D) node[midway,above left] {$s$};
\draw[dashed] (D)--(P) node[midway,right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$h$};
\draw[dashed] (C)--(Q);
\coordinate (p1) at ($(P)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\coordinate (p2) at ($(P)!0.3cm!(D)$);
\draw (p1) -- ($(p1)+(p2)-(P)$) -- (p2);
\node[above] at (2.5,2.6) {$a$};
\node[below] at (2.5,0) {$b$};
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(0,-1.2);
\draw[dashed] (1,0)--(1,-1.2);
\draw[dashed] (4,0)--(4,-1.2);
\draw[dashed] (5,0)--(5,-1.2);
\draw[<->] (0,-1.0)--(1,-1.0) node[midway,below] {$\frac{b-a}{2}$};
\draw[<->] (4,-1.0)--(5,-1.0) node[midway,below] {$\frac{b-a}{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The diagonals of a **kite or rhombus** cross at right angles. In a rhombus both diagonals bisect each other, so their halves form the shorter sides of a right-angled triangle. In a general kite, the symmetry diagonal bisects only the other diagonal; its own upper and lower segments need not be equal. In this kite the full diagonals are $x$ and $y=a+b$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (W) at (-1.6,0);
\coordinate (E) at (1.6,0);
\coordinate (N) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (S) at (0,-1.6);
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (W)--(N)--(E)--(S)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (W)--(O) node[midway,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{x}{2}$};
\draw[dashed] (O)--(E);
\draw[dashed] (O)--(N) node[midway,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$a$};
\draw[dashed] (O)--(S) node[midway,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$b$};
\coordinate (o1) at ($(O)!0.3cm!(E)$);
\coordinate (o2) at ($(O)!0.3cm!(N)$);
\draw (o1) -- ($(o1)+(o2)-(O)$) -- (o2);
\draw (N)--(E) node[midway,above right] {$s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

